[gd_scene load_steps=24 format=3]

; SQUAD AUTOMATIC — belt-fed, box-fed, carried.
;
; BUILT AT THE MARK ONE'S SCALE AND IN ITS AXES: barrel down +X, sights above
; +Y, about five units long so the same Holder scale of 0.26 puts it in the
; hand. That is not laziness — it means the ADS solve is the same arithmetic,
; and more importantly the two guns share ONE sight picture.
;
; THE SIGHT PICTURE IS THE M249's, not the Mark One's. The rifle's rear sight
; is an open window between two ears; this is a PEEP — a plate on the feed
; cover with a small hole punched through it and a windage knob on the right —
; and the front post stands between two tall squared ears rather than the
; rifle's low wings. Aperture 0.12 wide by 0.1 high against the front
; assembly's 0.166, so the ears sit inside the hole with daylight around them,
; which is what a SAW gunner sees.
;
; The HEIGHTS are still the house numbers: aperture centre 0.573 (y 0.523 to
; 0.623), front post tip 0.573, dead level. Read bolt_rifle_model.tscn's header
; for why flat is not optional — the shot is traced along the muzzle's +X, so
; any pitch used to level a rising sight line aims the bullet somewhere the
; sights are not. Changing the sight PICTURE must not change the sight LINE,
; which is why ads_position and ads_rotation are untouched by this.
;
; NOTHING MAY SIT ON THE y 0.573 / z 0 LINE BETWEEN x 1.3 AND x 4.4. The carry
; handle's posts used to, dead centre, 0.03 thick and completely opaque — the
; ADS view was the inside of a black tab. It is now a barrel-change handle low
; over the handguard, which is where an MG carries one anyway.
;
; Sight radius here is 3.1 units against the Mark One's 3.57, so the aperture
; sits slightly larger in view — correct for a weapon meant to be swung onto
; something close rather than aimed at something far.
;
; Palette from machine_gun_model.tscn. Detail density matched to the Mark One
; (34 parts): ribbed box, slotted handguard, belt links, sling loops, comb.

